<title>HUNGARY: TABLE-Central Europe Jan-May auto sales up 20.8 pc.</title>
<headline>TABLE-Central Europe Jan-May auto sales up 20.8 pc.</headline>
<dateline>BUDAPEST 1997-07-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>Sales of autos in Central European Free Trade Agreement (CEFTA) countries rose 20.8 percent to 409,288 units in January-to-May 1997 from 338,683 in January-to-May 1996, according to a Reuters survey.</p>
<p>CEFTA, which was formed in 1993, includes the Czech Republic, Hungary, Poland, Slovakia and Slovenia. The survey, which Reuters issues monthly, defines autos as cars, minibuses, pickups, vans and other four-wheeled motor vehicles not over 5.0 tonnes in gross weight.</p>
